    "result_text": "Held people and held claims now count in every total again. The only thing a hold still does is in the pools: a held person gets no cut, and their share is spread over the other members. I ran `bin/e2e` and all 4 acceptance tests passed. I changed one comment in the server's rollup code after that run had started; it was only a comment, so I didn't run the tests again.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Front end:**\n  - `liveHolds` in `features/people/people.ts` no longer includes a person's own holds.\n  - `counts` in `features/claims/claims.ts` and `countsInPeriod` in `features/periods/periods.ts` no longer leave out a held claim.\n  - Every total goes through these three functions, so all of them change: claimed, due-totals, overdue, period figures, the CSV export, search, the dashboard and the rest.\n- **Server:**\n  - In `person/Person.java`, `counts` and `heldOn` no longer look at holds or held claims.\n  - `period/Period.java` no longer leaves held claims out of the period figures.\n  - Statements, the rollup lines and period closing all go through these, so they now count held claims too.\n- **Pools:** unchanged. A member's \"held\" flag still comes from the pool query in `PoolRepository` (the SQL), so the pool cuts work as before.\n\nRetirement still works as it did. Once a person's retirement date is reached, their open claims leave the live totals unless a statement covers them. That rule used to be built on top of holds; it now applies only to retirement. Showing and storing holds (the `person-hold` and `claim-held` values, the hold/release actions and their audit records) is unchanged. No stored data or schema changed. I also updated the code comments that described the old rule.",
